    "message": {
     "content": "### The Short Answer\n\n**Do not accept this offer as-is, and do not walk away yet.** \n\nThis is a typical Google \"initial anchor\" offer. It is a solid baseline, but it leaves significant money on the table for a poached candidate from Meta. Because you are happy where you are and Google initiated contact, you hold substantial leverage. You should **negotiate aggressively**.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown vs. Your Meta Compensation\n\nAt first glance, dividing $1.08M by 4 gives an average of $270,000/year in stock, making the annualized total compensation appear to be **~$552,000/year** (only ~$26,000 above your current Meta run rate). \n\nHowever, Google does not vest equity straight-line. Google\u2019s standard GSU schedule is front-loaded:\n* **Year 1:** 38% ($410,400)\n* **Year 2:** 32% ($345,600)\n* **Year 3:** 20% ($216,000)\n* **Year 4:** 10% ($108,000)\n\n#### Year-by-Year Cash Flow Comparison (assuming target bonus & flat stock):\n\n| Year | Google Base + Bonus (20%) | Google Equity Vest | Google Total Comp (Offer) | Meta Total Comp (Current) | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $282,000 | $410,400 | **~$692,400** | ~$476,000* | **+$216,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $282,000 | $345,600 | **~$627,600** | ~$526,000 | **+$101,600** |\n| **Year 3** | $282,000 | $216,000 | **~$498,000** *(excl. refreshes)* | ~$526,000 | **-$28,000** |\n| **Year 4** | $282,000 | $108,000 | **~$390,000** *(excl. refreshes)* | ~$526,000 | **-$136,000** |\n\n*\\*Using your stated $200k next-12-months Meta vest.*\n\n### The Realities of This Offer\n1. **Strong Years 1 & 2, but a steep drop-off in Years 3 & 4:** While the recruiter mentioned stacking refreshers, Google refreshers typically vest evenly over 4 years (25%/yr) and depend strictly on performance ratings. They rarely completely offset the drop from 38% down to 10% without top-tier performance ratings.\n2. **Base Salary Pay Cut:** Google offered you **$235,000**, which is lower than your current $240,000 base. The top of their posted band is $271,000, meaning they have ample headroom.\n3. **No Sign-On Bonus:** It is standard practice in Big Tech to provide a sign-on bonus ($50,000\u2013$100,000+ at L6) when poaching passive talent from a peer company like Meta.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Negotiation Targets)\n\nGiven that Meta IC6 $\\leftrightarrow$ Google L6 is a lateral move, Google must pay a \"switching premium\" to get you to leave.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter-Offer | Why |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** | Recovers your current $240k base and stays within their $183k\u2013$271k band. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | Moves 4-year average equity from $270k/yr to ~$315k\u2013$325k/yr. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Replaces unvested/forfeited bonuses and offsets switching risk. |\n\n---\n\n### Negotiation Script & Strategy\n\nCall your recruiter (or reply via email) and frame the counter around being excited by the team, but financially unable to justify a lateral step without a premium.\n\n> *\"Thanks again for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team and see strong alignment with the product vision.*\n>\n> *I want to be transparent: because I wasn\u2019t actively looking and have strong momentum at Meta, the economics need to make clear sense for me to move. Looking at the offer:*\n>\n> 1. *The base of $235k is below my current $240k base.*\n> 2. *While the front-loaded equity structure makes Years 1 and 2 strong, the annualized average and the drop-off in Years 3 and 4 make this essentially a lateral compensation move against my steady-state equity at Meta.*\n>\n> *If we can bridge this with **$255,000 base salary**, an equity grant of **$1.25M**, and an upfront sign-on bonus of **$75,000**, I would be thrilled to sign immediately.\"*\n\n### Decision Framework\n* **If they meet your counter (or meet you halfway):** Total comp in Year 1 would reach **~$780k+** (with sign-on), and Year 2 would be **~$680k+**. You should strongly consider accepting.\n* **If they refuse to move entirely:** Walk away or stay at Meta. There is no reason to take on the onboarding risk, reset your internal network, and accept a lower base salary for an offer that becomes a pay cut by Year 3.",
