    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict: **You should strongly consider accepting this offer.**\n\nGoogle responded with what is essentially top-of-market for an L6 Product Manager. A **$200,000 upfront cash sign-on bonus** is rare at L6 and completely changes the financial calculus in your favor.\n\n---\n\n### The Updated Math: A Massive 2-Year Payout\n\nBecause Google front-loads equity (38% in Year 1, 32% in Year 2), combining that with a $200,000 cash sign-on creates an extraordinary near-term cash flow:\n\n| Compensation Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 (Base + Target) | Year 4 (Base + Target) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 |\n| **New-Hire RSUs** | $410,400 *(38%)* | $345,600 *(32%)* | $216,000 *(20%)* | $108,000 *(10%)* |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | **$200,000** | $0 | $0 | $0 |\n| **Google Total Comp** | **~$898,400** | **~$633,600** | **~$504,000\\*** | **~$396,000\\*** |\n| **Meta Comparison** | ~$476,000 | ~$526,000 | ~$526,000 | ~$526,000 |\n| **Net Difference** | <span style=\"color:green\">**+$422,400**</span> | <span style=\"color:green\">**+$107,600**</span> | <span style=\"color:red\">**-$22,000\\***</span> | <span style=\"color:red\">**-$130,000\\***</span> |\n\n*\\*Note: Years 3 and 4 at Google do not account for annual stock refresh grants or merit increases, which will narrow/eliminate that gap.*\n\n#### The Cumulative Impact:\n* **Over 2 Years:** You will earn **~$1,532,000** at Google vs. **~$1,002,000** at Meta. That is an extra **+$530,000 in your pocket** over 24 months.\n* **Over 4 Years (Guaranteed baseline):** You are guaranteed **$2,432,000** at Google vs. **~$2,054,000** at Meta, averaging **$608,000/year** even if your performance refreshers were zero.\n\n---\n\n### Why Google Structured It This Way\nGoogle\u2019s compensation committee has hard caps on new-hire equity bands for L6 (typically topping out right around $1M\u2013$1.1M). Instead of breaking the equity band, the recruiter pulled the **cash sign-on lever** to bridge the $200k you would leave behind at Meta.\n\n---\n\n### How to Play This: The \"2-Year Arbitrage\" Playbook\n\n1. **Take the Money in Years 1 & 2:** You pocket an outsized $1.53M over the next two years. \n2. **Re-evaluate in Year 3:** \n   * If you perform well and secure strong refresh grants or an L7 promotion, your total comp will stay comfortably above $600k\u2013$700k+.\n   * If refreshers are underwhelming or you prefer Meta\u2019s culture/pace, you can **boomerang back to Meta** at IC7 or an elevated IC6 package with your Google cash already banked.\n\n---\n\n### 3 Final Details to Check Before You Sign\n\n1. **Sign-On Clawback Terms:** Check whether the $200,000 has a 12-month or 24-month clawback. A standard Google sign-on is a 12-month prorated repayment clause (meaning if you leave after 12 months, you keep 100% of it).\n2. **Timing Your Exit:** Coordinate your start date so you do not leave just days before an upcoming Meta bonus payout or quarterly equity vest.\n3. **Written Confirmation:** Ensure the final offer letter explicitly lists the **$240,000 base**, **$1,080,000 GSU grant**, and the **$200,000 sign-on bonus**.",
